\subsection{System Model and Threat Model}
\label{sec:system_model}

\subsubsection{Participants and Communication Model}
We consider $N$ clients, each holding a private dataset $\mathcal{D}_i$, and a central server. Communication consists of one upload phase wherein each client encrypts per-block teacher input--output pairs under CKKS and uploads ciphertexts to the server. This constitutes the only required client--server interaction during training; no additional client interaction occurs during the server-side training loop. Following training, an optional release phase allows clients to collectively perform threshold decryption to obtain the trained student model in plaintext. If omitted, the student remains encrypted for direct encrypted inference.

No additional client interaction is required during the server-side training loop.

\subsubsection{Threat Model}
We assume a \textit{semi-honest} (honest-but-curious) server that follows the protocol but may attempt to infer information about client data. Clients are assumed honest.

The server never observes any plaintext data at any point during training. Client inputs, teacher outputs, student weights, student activations, loss values, and gradients are all ciphertext from the server's perspective. The server functions purely as a blind compute delegate that executes encrypted operations according to the protocol specification provided by the clients.

For additional protection, clients can add calibrated differential privacy noise before encryption, achieving $(\epsilon, \delta)$-differential privacy. This is optional, since the cryptographic guarantee already prevents the server from inspecting any individual contribution.

\subsubsection{Encryption Parameters}
\label{sec:encryption_params}

Encryption parameters depend on the phase of the protocol.

\textbf{During training}, each block is trained independently with fresh ciphertexts (Section~\ref{sec:block_training}). The multiplicative depth required per block depends on the block's internal structure. For a single PolyBasicBlock containing two degree-2 polynomial activations, the forward pass consumes 2 multiplicative levels and the backward pass consumes additional levels for the chain rule through the polynomial terms. Since each block receives fresh ciphertexts and operates independently, there is no level accumulation across blocks.

\textbf{During encrypted inference} (post-deployment), the full student model requires 17 multiplicative levels for a complete forward pass (Section~\ref{sec:ckks_budget}), necessitating $\log N = 14$ (ring dimension 16384), 18 coefficient modulus primes, and approximately 1.2~MB ciphertexts.

\textbf{At model release}, if clients elect threshold decryption, the student model weights are decrypted collaboratively and the model can subsequently be used for plaintext inference.

\subsection{Phase 1: Client-Side Local Training}
\label{sec:phase1}

Each client $i$ trains a local ResNet-18 teacher $T_i$ on their private partition $\mathcal{D}_i$ using standard supervised learning with ReLU activations and batch normalisation. This phase is fully local---no data leaves the client device.

\textbf{Shared initialisation.} The server distributes a common random initialisation $\theta_0$ to all clients before local training begins. Each client fine-tunes from $\theta_0$ on their private data. This is standard practice in federated learning and does not constitute a privacy violation: $\theta_0$ is randomly generated and carries no information about any client's data.

\textbf{Data partitioning.} Data is partitioned across clients using a Dirichlet distribution with concentration parameter $\alpha$, where $\alpha \to 0$ produces extreme non-IID partitions (each client sees few classes) and $\alpha \to \infty$ produces IID partitions. Our experiments evaluate $\alpha \in \{0.1, 0.5, 1.0, 100.0\}$ across $N \in \{1, 2, 4, 8, 16\}$ clients.

\subsection{Phase 2: Per-Block Feature Extraction and Encrypted Upload}
\label{sec:phase2}

After local training, each client extracts teacher representations at every block boundary, computes normalisation statistics, and uploads encrypted input--output pairs to the server.

\subsubsection{Block Boundary Definitions}
\label{sec:block_boundaries}

We define six blocks matching the teacher and student architectures. Block 0 (stem) takes raw images as input and outputs the result post-conv1, batch normalisation, and ReLU. Block 1 (layer1) takes the stem output and yields features after two BasicBlocks. Blocks 2 through 4 correspond to layers 2 through 4, respectively, incorporating spatial downsampling. Finally, Block 5 (head) processes layer 4 outputs into class logits via average pooling and a fully-connected layer. For each defined block $k$, client $i$ extracts the tuple $(\textbf{x}_k, \textbf{y}_k)$, corresponding respectively to the block's input and output when running the teacher model on local data $\mathcal{D}_i$.

\subsubsection{Local Data Protocol}
\label{sec:local_data}

Each client generates teacher outputs on their \textit{own local training data}~$\mathcal{D}_i$, not on a shared auxiliary dataset. This design has two advantages.

First, each teacher evaluates samples from its own training distribution, producing high-confidence supervision signals for the classes it has learned well. A client that specialises in a particular subset of classes provides strong logits and features for those classes, rather than uncertain outputs for classes it has not seen.

Second, by using each client's data independently---without averaging or aggregation---the signal from every individual client is preserved at full strength. Under the auxiliary-data protocol (evaluated as a comparison baseline in Section~\ref{sec:experiments}), averaging dilutes the useful signal from specialist clients with weak contributions from clients that have not seen the relevant classes.

\subsubsection{Client-Side Collaborative Normalisation}
\label{sec:collab_norm}

Before encryption, each client computes per-channel mean and standard deviation over their block-$k$ outputs:
\begin{equation}
    \mu_c^{(k,i)} = \mathbb{E}_{x \in \mathcal{D}_i}\!\left[ y_{k,c}(x) \right], \quad
    \sigma_c^{(k,i)} = \text{Std}_{x \in \mathcal{D}_i}\!\left[ y_{k,c}(x) \right]
    \label{eq:client_stats}
\end{equation}
for each channel $c$, and normalises their targets to zero mean and unit variance per channel before encryption:
\begin{equation}
    \tilde{y}_{k,c} = \frac{y_{k,c} - \mu_c^{(k,i)}}{\sigma_c^{(k,i)}}
    \label{eq:client_norm}
\end{equation}

This normalisation is a core component of the protocol, not an optional enhancement. Without it, teachers trained on different data distributions produce features with incompatible magnitude ranges. In our experiments, client-side normalisation eliminates NaN divergence that otherwise occurs at layer3 for $N \geq 4$ clients, and improves student accuracy from ${\sim}$19\% to ${\sim}$37\% at $N{=}4$, $\alpha{=}0.5$ (Section~\ref{sec:experiments}).

The communication overhead is minimal: $2C$ floating-point values per block per client, where $C$ is the channel count. These statistics can be sent in plaintext (they are aggregate statistics over the client's data, not individual samples) or encrypted if stricter privacy is required.

\subsubsection{Communication Cost}
\label{sec:communication}

For each block $k$, client $i$ uploads encrypted input--output pairs for a subset of their local data. With CKKS parameters $\log N = 13$ and 4096 slots per ciphertext, the per-client upload cost depends on the feature dimensions at each block boundary.

Knowledge distillation is data-efficient: in our experiments, using 20--25\% of each client's local data provides sufficient supervision. The upload is one-shot---no further client interaction is needed during the server-side training loop.

\subsection{Phase 3: Server-Side Block-Level Encrypted Distillation}
\label{sec:phase3}

The server trains the HE-compatible student model block by block. Each block is trained independently with its own fresh ciphertexts, and blocks are composed into the full model after all training is complete.

\subsubsection{Block-Level Training}
\label{sec:block_training}

For each block $k \in \{0, \ldots, 5\}$, the server proceeds as follows:

\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Receive fresh ciphertexts.} The server holds encrypted input--output pairs $\left(\enc{x_k^{(i,j)}},\, \enc{\tilde{y}_k^{(i,j)}}\right)$ from each client $i$, for samples $j \in \mathcal{D}_i$.
    
    \item \textbf{Sequential multi-teacher training.} The server trains student block $k$ on each client's data sequentially: first all of client 1's pairs, then client 2's, and so on. In practice, all clients' pairs are pooled and shuffled to avoid loss discontinuities at client boundaries. Each gradient step updates the (encrypted) student weights for block $k$.
    
    \item \textbf{Freeze and proceed.} After training, block $k$'s weights are frozen (remain as ciphertext). The server moves to block $k{+}1$ with fresh ciphertexts.
\end{enumerate}

\textbf{No averaging, no aggregation.} Unlike federated averaging approaches, the server does not combine teacher outputs from different clients. Each client's data provides independent gradient steps that accumulate learning into the same set of student block weights. A client with high accuracy on birds contributes strong bird supervision; a client with high accuracy on trucks contributes strong truck supervision. The student learns from both without either signal being diluted.

\textbf{Fresh ciphertexts per block.} Each block starts training with fresh encrypted input--output pairs from the clients. This is the key design choice that avoids multiplicative level accumulation across blocks. Block $k$'s training consumes a fixed number of CKKS levels determined only by block $k$'s internal structure, regardless of how many blocks precede it.

\subsubsection{Student-Input Training}
\label{sec:student_input}

For block $k > 0$, the input to block $k$ during training must match what block $k$ will receive at inference. At inference, block $k$ receives the output of the student's frozen blocks $0 \to (k{-}1)$, not teacher features. If we train block $k$ on teacher-produced inputs, the train--inference distribution gap causes the composed model to collapse to near-random accuracy.

We therefore use \textit{student-input training}: for each training sample, the server runs the student's frozen blocks $0 \to (k{-}1)$ on the encrypted input image to produce the actual input that block $k$ will see at inference. The teacher-produced output $\enc{\tilde{y}_k}$ serves as the target.

For block 0, the input is the raw encrypted image (no preceding student blocks).

\textbf{CKKS depth implication.} Student-input training requires a forward pass through frozen blocks $0 \to (k{-}1)$ to produce the input for block $k$. This accumulates multiplicative levels across the frozen chain. For block 4, the frozen forward pass consumes approximately 13 levels before block 4's own training begins. This is manageable within CKKS parameter budgets for moderate-depth models, but represents a trade-off between train--inference consistency and encrypted computation cost that we discuss further in Section~\ref{sec:ckks_budget}.

\subsubsection{Loss Function}
\label{sec:loss}

Each block is trained with a magnitude-regularised normalised MSE loss.

\textbf{Normalised MSE.} Student and teacher features are normalised per channel to zero mean and unit variance before computing MSE:
\begin{equation}
    \mathcal{L}_{\text{NormMSE}} = \frac{1}{C} \sum_{c=1}^{C} \text{MSE}\!\left(\frac{\hat{f}_c - \mu_{\hat{f}_c}}{\sigma_{\hat{f}_c}},\; \frac{\tilde{f}_c - \mu_{\tilde{f}_c}}{\sigma_{\tilde{f}_c}}\right)
    \label{eq:normmse}
\end{equation}
where $\hat{f}_c$ and $\tilde{f}_c$ are the student prediction and normalised teacher target for channel $c$. This scale-invariant loss handles the distributional mismatch between PolyAct student activations (signed, potentially large) and ReLU teacher activations (non-negative, bounded).

\textbf{Magnitude regularisation.} NormMSE alone is scale-invariant and permits the student to grow magnitudes without penalty. Since subsequent frozen polynomial blocks amplify any scale drift, we add a log-ratio penalty:
\begin{equation}
    \mathcal{L}_{\text{MagReg}} = \mathcal{L}_{\text{NormMSE}} + \lambda \cdot \frac{1}{C} \sum_{c=1}^{C} \left(\log \frac{\sigma_{\hat{f}_c}}{\sigma_{\tilde{f}_c}}\right)^{\!2}
    \label{eq:magreg}
\end{equation}
The log-ratio form is symmetric (penalises student magnitudes that are too large or too small equally) and scale-free (proportional to the multiplicative mismatch). The regularisation weight $\lambda$ scales with block depth: $\lambda = 2$ for early blocks, increasing to $\lambda = 6$ for deeper blocks where magnitude accumulation from the frozen chain is more severe.

\textbf{MSE on logits.} For the head block (block 5), which outputs class logits, we use plain MSE without normalisation or magnitude regularisation, since logits are low-dimensional and do not exhibit the spatial channel structure of intermediate features.

We use MSE rather than KL divergence because softmax is not a polynomial operation and cannot be evaluated under CKKS encryption. MSE on raw logits is HE-compatible: the squaring operation requires one ciphertext--ciphertext multiplication (1 CKKS level), and the remaining subtraction and averaging are level-free.

\subsection{Protocol Summary}
\label{sec:algorithm}

Algorithm~\ref{alg:heifd} summarises the complete HEIFD protocol.

\begin{algorithm}[t]
\caption{HEIFD: HE-Based Intermediate Feature Distillation}
\label{alg:heifd}
\begin{algorithmic}[1]
\REQUIRE $N$ clients with private datasets $\mathcal{D}_1, \ldots, \mathcal{D}_N$; HE-compatible student architecture $S$ with $K{+}1$ blocks
\ENSURE Trained student model $S$ (encrypted or decrypted)

\STATE \textbf{--- Phase 1: Client-Side (parallel, local) ---}
\FOR{each client $i = 1, \ldots, N$ \textbf{in parallel}}
    \STATE Train teacher $T_i$ on $\mathcal{D}_i$ via standard SGD
    \FOR{each block $k = 0, \ldots, K$}
        \STATE Extract input--output pairs $(\mathbf{x}_k^{(i,j)}, \mathbf{y}_k^{(i,j)})$ by running $T_i$ on $\mathcal{D}_i$
        \STATE Compute per-channel statistics: $\mu_c^{(k,i)}, \sigma_c^{(k,i)}$ over $\mathbf{y}_k$ \hfill\COMMENT{Eq.~\ref{eq:client_stats}}
        \STATE Normalise targets: $\tilde{\mathbf{y}}_k^{(i,j)} \leftarrow (\mathbf{y}_k^{(i,j)} - \mu) / \sigma$ \hfill\COMMENT{Eq.~\ref{eq:client_norm}}
    \ENDFOR
    \STATE Encrypt all pairs under CKKS; upload $\{\enc{\mathbf{x}_k^{(i,j)}}, \enc{\tilde{\mathbf{y}}_k^{(i,j)}}\}$ to server
\ENDFOR

\STATE
\STATE \textbf{--- Phase 2: Server-Side Block Distillation ---}
\STATE Initialise student $S$ with near-linear PolyAct ($a{=}0.3, b{=}0.7, c{=}0$)
\FOR{block $k = 0, \ldots, K$}
    \STATE Pool encrypted pairs from all $N$ clients for block $k$; shuffle
    \STATE Set $\lambda_k \leftarrow \lambda_{\text{base}} \cdot (1 + 2 \cdot k/K)$ \hfill\COMMENT{deeper blocks: stronger MagReg}
    \STATE Set $\eta_k \leftarrow \eta_{\text{base}} \cdot (1 - 0.8 \cdot k/K)$ \hfill\COMMENT{deeper blocks: lower LR}
    \FOR{epoch $= 1, \ldots, E$}
        \FOR{each mini-batch $(\enc{\mathbf{x}}, \enc{\tilde{\mathbf{y}}_k})$}
            \IF{$k = 0$}
                \STATE $\mathbf{h} \leftarrow \enc{\mathbf{x}}$ \hfill\COMMENT{raw image input}
            \ELSE
                \STATE $\mathbf{h} \leftarrow S_{\text{blocks}[0:k{-}1]}(\enc{\mathbf{x}})$ \hfill\COMMENT{student-input, frozen prefix}
            \ENDIF
            \STATE $\hat{\mathbf{y}} \leftarrow S_{\text{block}[k]}(\mathbf{h})$ \hfill\COMMENT{current block forward}
            \IF{$k < K$}
                \STATE $\mathcal{L} \leftarrow \mathcal{L}_{\text{NormMSE}}(\hat{\mathbf{y}}, \enc{\tilde{\mathbf{y}}_k}) + \lambda_k \cdot \mathcal{L}_{\text{MagReg}}(\hat{\mathbf{y}}, \enc{\tilde{\mathbf{y}}_k})$ \hfill\COMMENT{Eq.~\ref{eq:magreg}}
            \ELSE
                \STATE $\mathcal{L} \leftarrow \text{MSE}(\hat{\mathbf{y}}, \enc{\tilde{\mathbf{y}}_k})$ \hfill\COMMENT{logit block}
            \ENDIF
            \STATE Update $S_{\text{block}[k]}$ via encrypted gradient descent with learning rate $\eta_k$
        \ENDFOR
    \ENDFOR
    \STATE Freeze $S_{\text{block}[k]}$
\ENDFOR

\STATE
\STATE \textbf{--- Phase 3 (optional): Model Release ---}
\STATE Clients collaboratively perform threshold decryption to obtain $S$ in plaintext
\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}

\subsection{HE-Compatible Student Architecture}
\label{sec:architecture}

The student is a PolyResNet-18: structurally identical to the teacher ResNet-18 but with all HE-incompatible operations replaced. This structural correspondence enables block-level feature matching between teacher and student.

\subsubsection{Component Replacements}

\textbf{ReLU $\to$ PolyAct.} ReLU is a comparison operation ($\max(0, x)$) with no efficient polynomial representation under CKKS. We replace it with a learnable degree-2 polynomial:
\begin{equation}
    \text{PolyAct}(x) = ax^2 + bx + c
    \label{eq:polyact}
\end{equation}
initialised at $a = 0.3$, $b = 0.7$, $c = 0$, so that the initial behaviour is approximately linear ($\text{PolyAct}(x) \approx 0.7x$ for small $x$). The coefficients are learnable and adapt during training; after training they are frozen as plaintext constants.

Under CKKS, $x^2$ is one ciphertext--ciphertext multiplication (1 multiplicative level). The remaining terms ($ax^2, bx, c$) involve only plaintext--ciphertext operations (0 levels). Total cost: \textbf{1 multiplicative level per activation}.

\textbf{BatchNorm $\to$ ChannelScale.} Batch normalisation requires computing running means and variances, which involve division ($x / \sigma$) requiring iterative Goldschmidt approximation at a cost of ${\sim}$10 multiplicative levels per invocation. We replace it with a per-channel affine transform:
\begin{equation}
    \text{ChannelScale}(x) = \gamma \odot x + \beta
    \label{eq:channelscale}
\end{equation}
with learnable $\gamma$ (initialised to 1) and $\beta$ (initialised to 0). Since both are known plaintext at inference, this costs \textbf{0 multiplicative levels}.

\textbf{MaxPool $\to$ removed.} Max pooling is a comparison operation. For CIFAR-10 (32$\times$32 input), the initial max pool in standard ResNet-18 is unnecessary and is replaced by an identity operation. Spatial downsampling is handled by stride-2 convolutions in layers 2--4.

\subsubsection{PolyBasicBlock Structure}

Each PolyBasicBlockV2 follows the dataflow:
\begin{equation}
    x \xrightarrow{\text{conv}_1} \xrightarrow{\text{CS}_1} \xrightarrow{\text{PolyAct}_1} \xrightarrow{\text{conv}_2} \xrightarrow{\text{CS}_2} \xrightarrow{+\,\text{identity}} \xrightarrow{\text{PolyAct}_2} y
\end{equation}
Convolutions, ChannelScale, and the residual addition consume 0 levels. The two PolyAct invocations consume 1 level each, for a total of \textbf{2 multiplicative levels per block}.

\subsubsection{CKKS Inference Depth Budget}
\label{sec:ckks_budget}

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\caption{CKKS multiplicative levels for PolyResNet-18 encrypted inference}
\label{tab:ckks_levels}
\begin{tabular}{l|c|c|c}
\hline
\textbf{Component} & \textbf{Levels/Block} & \textbf{Blocks} & \textbf{Total} \\
\hline
Stem (conv $+$ CS $+$ PolyAct) & --- & 1 & 1 \\
Layer 1 (2 PolyBasicBlocks) & 2 & 2 & 4 \\
Layer 2 & 2 & 2 & 4 \\
Layer 3 & 2 & 2 & 4 \\
Layer 4 & 2 & 2 & 4 \\
AvgPool $+$ FC & --- & --- & 0 \\
\hline
\textbf{Total} & & & \textbf{17} \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

At inference, model weights are frozen plaintext constants. All operations involving weights (convolution, ChannelScale, FC) are plaintext--ciphertext and consume 0 levels. Only PolyAct's $x^2$ term consumes levels.

During \textit{encrypted training}, server weights become ciphertext after the first gradient update involving encrypted targets. Operations previously marked PT$\times$CT become CT$\times$CT (1 additional level each). However, because each block trains independently with fresh ciphertexts, this increased cost is confined to the current block's depth budget and does not accumulate across the full network.

\subsubsection{Operation-Level CKKS Costs}

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\caption{CKKS costs per operation. At inference, weights are plaintext (PT) and activations are ciphertext (CT). During training, weights become CT after the first encrypted gradient update.}
\label{tab:he_ops}
\begin{tabular}{l|l|c|c}
\hline
\textbf{Operation} & \textbf{Inference} & \textbf{Inf.\ Levels} & \textbf{Train Levels} \\
\hline
Convolution & PT$\times$CT & 0 & 1 \\
ChannelScale & PT$\times$CT & 0 & 1 \\
PolyAct ($ax^2{+}bx{+}c$) & CT$\times$CT & 1 & 1 \\
Residual add & CT$+$CT & 0 & 0 \\
Average pooling & $\Sigma$CT $\times$ PT & 0 & 0 \\
FC layer & PT$\times$CT & 0 & 1 \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\subsection{Training Stability}
\label{sec:stability}

Polynomial activations introduce unique training challenges that require multiple interlocking stability mechanisms.

\subsubsection{Magnitude Accumulation}
The quadratic term in PolyAct amplifies activation magnitudes at every composition. With $\text{PolyAct}(x) = 0.3x^2 + 0.7x$, an input of magnitude 5 produces output ${\sim}$11; a further composition gives ${\sim}$44. After 5 compositions (typical by layer3), magnitudes can reach $10^4$, causing NaN divergence. This problem is exacerbated under multi-client settings ($N \geq 4$), where heterogeneous client distributions increase feature variance through the frozen chain.

\subsubsection{Stability Mechanisms}
\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Client-side normalisation} (Eq.~\ref{eq:client_norm}) bounds the target range before encryption, preventing magnitude mismatch across clients.
    \item \textbf{Magnitude-regularised loss} (Eq.~\ref{eq:magreg}) constrains student feature scales to match teacher targets at each block, preventing unbounded growth through subsequent frozen polynomial blocks.
    \item \textbf{ChannelScale} provides per-channel affine capacity that partially compensates for the missing variance normalisation of batch normalisation.
    \item \textbf{Gradient clipping} ($\text{max\_norm} = 1.0$) prevents gradient explosion from the quadratic term.
    \item \textbf{Student-input training} (Section~\ref{sec:student_input}) ensures each block trains on the distribution it will receive at inference, eliminating the train--inference gap that causes teacher-input approaches to collapse to random accuracy.
    \item \textbf{Block-independent training} with fresh ciphertexts prevents CKKS level accumulation and allows each block to start from a clean numerical state.
    \item \textbf{Per-block learning rate scaling}: deeper blocks use lower learning rates (down to $0.2\times$ base for layer4) since they are more sensitive to magnitude perturbations from the longer frozen chain preceding them.
\end{enumerate}

Our ablation study (Section~\ref{sec:experiments}) isolates the contribution of each mechanism. In particular, removing client-side normalisation causes NaN divergence for $N \geq 4$; removing the magnitude regularisation penalty degrades accuracy by ${\sim}$18\%; and replacing student-input with teacher-input training collapses accuracy from ${\sim}$71\% to ${\sim}$12\%.

\subsection{Privacy Analysis}
\label{sec:security}

We provide a formal privacy argument for the HEIFD protocol.

\textbf{Definition: Threat Model}
The server $\mathcal{S}$ is \textit{semi-honest} (honest-but-curious): it follows the protocol specification exactly but may attempt to infer information about client data from any quantity it observes during execution. All $N$ clients are assumed honest. The server holds no plaintext data at any point.


\textbf{Definition: Server's View}

During protocol execution, the server observes:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item CKKS ciphertexts: $\{\enc{\mathbf{x}_k^{(i,j)}}, \enc{\tilde{\mathbf{y}}_k^{(i,j)}}\}$ for all clients $i$, blocks $k$, and samples $j$.
    \item Normalisation statistics: per-channel $(\mu_c^{(k,i)}, \sigma_c^{(k,i)})$ for each client $i$ and block $k$ (optionally encrypted).
    \item Protocol metadata: number of clients $N$, number of samples per client $|\mathcal{D}_i|$, tensor dimensions.
\end{enumerate}
All intermediate quantities---student weights, activations, loss values, gradients---are derived from encrypted operations on the above inputs and are therefore also ciphertexts.


\textbf{Theorem: Training-Time Privacy}
\label{thm:privacy}
Under the Ring Learning with Errors (RLWE) assumption with security parameter $\lambda \geq 128$, the HEIFD protocol reveals no information about individual client data samples $(x, y) \in \mathcal{D}_i$ to the server during training, beyond the protocol metadata (tensor dimensions and sample counts).


\textbf{Proof sketch.}

\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{CKKS IND-CPA security.} Each CKKS ciphertext $\enc{m}$ is computationally indistinguishable from $\enc{m'}$ for any messages $m, m'$ of the same dimension, under the RLWE assumption~\cite{cheon2017ckks}. The server cannot distinguish the encrypted features of one training sample from another, nor distinguish real features from random noise of the same shape.

    \item \textbf{Closure under homomorphic operations.} The server performs a sequence of homomorphic operations on the received ciphertexts: additions (CT$+$CT), multiplications (CT$\times$CT for PolyAct; PT$\times$CT for convolutions with encrypted weights), and scalar operations. Each operation maps ciphertexts to ciphertexts. The output of any sub-computation remains encrypted and inherits the IND-CPA guarantee of the input ciphertexts.

    \item \textbf{Simulatability.} The server's complete transcript---received ciphertexts, intermediate ciphertexts produced during training, and the final encrypted student model---can be simulated given only the public encryption parameters and the metadata (tensor dimensions, sample counts). A simulator generates random RLWE ciphertexts of the correct dimensions; by IND-CPA security, the server cannot distinguish the simulated transcript from the real one. Therefore, the server learns nothing beyond what is deducible from the metadata.

    \item \textbf{Normalisation statistics.} If per-channel statistics $(\mu_c, \sigma_c)$ are sent in plaintext, they are aggregate statistics computed over the client's entire local dataset. For a dataset of $n$ samples, each individual sample's contribution to $\mu_c$ and $\sigma_c$ is $O(1/n)$, providing natural statistical privacy. For stricter protection, these statistics can also be encrypted.
\end{enumerate}

\textbf{Post-decryption considerations.} If clients elect threshold decryption (Phase~3), the student model weights become plaintext. The released model, like any machine learning model, may be subject to membership inference attacks (MIA)~\cite{shokri2017membership, carlini2022membership}. This is a property of model release, not of the training protocol. Differential privacy noise can be added by clients before encryption to provide formal guarantees on the released model, composable with the cryptographic training-time guarantee.

\textbf{Comparison with differential privacy.} HE and DP provide complementary guarantees:
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{HE} provides a \textit{computational} guarantee during training: breaking it requires solving RLWE (conjectured hard even for quantum adversaries). The guarantee is non-degrading---it does not weaken with the number of training iterations---and introduces zero noise to the training process.
    \item \textbf{DP} provides an \textit{information-theoretic} guarantee on the released model, but with a privacy budget $\epsilon$ that degrades under composition (more training steps $\Rightarrow$ weaker guarantee or more noise). DP also introduces noise that monotonically degrades model utility with stronger privacy.
    \item In our protocol, HE protects the training process; DP can optionally protect the released model. The two mechanisms compose naturally: clients add calibrated noise before encryption, achieving $(\epsilon, \delta)$-DP on the released student without modifying the encrypted training protocol.
\end{itemize}

